---
import CommandOptions from '../../components/CommandOptions.astro';
import ProductPost from '../../components/ProductPost.astro';
import StringproofDemo from '../../components/StringproofDemo.astro';

const title = 'Stringproof';
const description = 'Catch damaged translations before they ship.';
const sourceUrl = 'https://github.com/jmonster/workcell/tree/main/stringproof';
const agentInstructionsUrl = '/stringproof/llms.txt';
const commandOptions = [
  {
    value: '0',
    summary: 'is clean.',
    detail: 'With no findings, the default command prints nothing.',
  },
  {
    value: '1',
    summary: 'means findings.',
    detail: 'Syntax, keys, placeholders, encoding, prompt residue, script contamination, and suspicious unchanged text are covered.',
  },
  {
    value: '2',
    summary: 'means the check could not run.',
    detail: 'Invalid invocation, unreadable input, and internal failures go to stderr.',
  },
];
---

<ProductPost
  title={title}
  description={description}
  product="stringproof"
  sourceUrl={sourceUrl}
  agentInstructionsUrl={agentInstructionsUrl}
  publishedAt="2026-08-05T00:00:00-04:00"
  publishedLabel="Aug 5, 2026"
>
  <h2>What’s it for?</h2>

  <p class="drop-cap">Translation output can parse cleanly and still be broken. Placeholders disappear, scripts mix, prompt instructions leak into values, and encoding damage survives review.</p>

  <p>Stringproof compares every Apple <code>.strings</code> target with its source and reports the damage. It is deterministic, local, and read-only: it validates translations but never translates or repairs them.</p>

  <div class="wide-demo" id="demonstration">
    <StringproofDemo />
  </div>

  <h2 id="use-it">Install and use</h2>

  <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://workcell-137.pages.dev/stringproof/install.sh | sh</code></pre>

  <h3>or tell your agent</h3>

  <pre><code>install https://workcell-137.pages.dev{agentInstructionsUrl}</code></pre>

  <p>then check the directory that contains the locale folders:</p>

  <pre><code>stringproof check ios --source-locale en</code></pre>

  <h3>or return stable JSON</h3>

  <pre><code>stringproof check ios --source-locale en --json</code></pre>

  <p>Stringproof finds Apple <code>.strings</code> catalogs, pairs each target with its source by relative path, and checks their keys and values.</p>

  <CommandOptions items={commandOptions} />

  <p id="limits"><strong>Limits.</strong> Stringproof can show that catalogs line up and that known corruption patterns are absent. It cannot show that a translation is correct, natural, or appropriate. It may flag a legitimate unchanged name, miss source text after a small alteration, or miss an entirely absent target catalog.</p>

  <p>Stringproof supports Apple <code>.strings</code> only. It uses no credentials, network access, model calls, configuration, or repair mode. <a href={sourceUrl}>View Stringproof on GitHub ↗</a></p>
</ProductPost>
